  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 342 PM CDT Wed Jun 17 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nAs of this afternoon, northwest to zonal flow remains over the \nnorthern reaches of CONUS, which is forecasted to continue into\nthe weekend. This will leave our FA stuck in below average \ntemperatures with a few weak waves passing through. The most \nnotable of these waves arrives Thursday night into Friday. This\nforcing, when combined with instability ranging up to 1000 \nJ/Kg, will produce widespread shower and thunderstorm activity, \nmuch as we are seeing today. Severe weather is not anticipated.\n\nInto next week, ridging will become more pronounced. This will\nfinally lead to a warming trend more reminiscent of mid to late\nJune with temperatures returning to the mid to upper 70s. While\nwarmer, this is still slightly below average for late June. \n\n...Showers and Thunder Today...\n\nUnder the influence of cyclonic flow and with about 1000 J/Kg of\nMUCAPE to work with, much of the region has seen afternoon \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms develop. These showers and \nstorms will continue throughout the afternoon and early evening \nhours, coming to an end as we lose daytime heating. Due to the \nlimited instability and under 20 knots of effective shear, \nsevere weather is not anticipated. However, those with outdoor \nactivities should be prepared for a few rumbles of thunder \nthrough about sunset.",
